#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115029 :question/id #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" :question/math-academy-id "e-4101" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2,$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x-3} & ={9}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={({3}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={3}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-3 & =2x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213 :topic/math-academy-id 343 :topic/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697 :topic/math-academy-id 1224 :topic/title "The Power Rule for Exponents"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81875651-c8fd-5a58-8470-f042f72ec368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112717} {:answer/id #uuid "9a477108-f8b7-55bb-a2bf-e77b0f9f55e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112718} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d397ee-aece-5c36-9380-f034cd4661ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112719} {:answer/id #uuid "489ab8bb-5c67-502e-9b56-cb727f4eae18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112720} {:answer/id #uuid "65d424ed-b5fb-5267-a2a3-b45c50a2e3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112721}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cba2d1e5-19c0-513f-a837-9f68dee89e73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112716}] :question/id #uuid "3c8224ab-cc4c-5aca-8c34-d9137bc80aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-26817" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $2^{x - 1} = 16^{x + 1}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8225087b-e8c7-5623-8f04-c786d8fe2ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114602} {:answer/id #uuid "cb105091-0465-5b4f-8b2b-de768d34d914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114603} {:answer/id #uuid "f0300825-591d-58ec-805d-f02475b88e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114604} {:answer/id #uuid "2646cf8e-ddcd-5c4b-8926-278b5cd16843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114605} {:answer/id #uuid "548cfdfb-7b17-56b2-a16b-d79dd1cef53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114606}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1204c0-675a-5a63-998d-c48c12991b31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114601}] :question/id #uuid "56c76940-04f5-5e86-a2ee-8e233a9a7e77" :question/math-academy-id "q-66877" :question/problem "Find the value of $h$ that satisfies the equation $12^{h - 4} = 144^{3h}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233135 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0af1043f-1c07-53a4-b1ab-ba4f9a319435" :question/math-academy-id "q-110539" :question/problem "If ${81}^{2a}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $81$ as ${3}^{4},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{81}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {({3}^{4})}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {3}^{8a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{8a} & ={3}^{-(a-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a & =-(a-1) \\\\ 8a & =-a+1 \\\\ 9a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234212 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9f2c7d0-b50b-5700-86ab-43258803d63b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66864" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{x-5}={3}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${3}^{2},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {({3}^{2})}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{2(x-5)} & ={3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5) & =x \\\\ 2x-10 & =x \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064573a3-946f-50fc-bb6d-8db167599b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235840} {:answer/id #uuid "196b0bfc-1a75-545a-b6d1-9a1d9f21aa6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235976} {:answer/id #uuid "952b585e-21b7-58c6-9366-29c782c98495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236763} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d411fb-c9aa-5f28-899e-0d22c425d2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237388} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1f2332-6cd9-5ad0-a46d-34ee3b988d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8d411fb-c9aa-5f28-899e-0d22c425d2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237388} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b48e037-f3fb-57d0-bb74-3cbcf8c6588e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237645}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47c3c6e7-da82-558f-8732-889800a1c1ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-100449" :question/problem "If ${8}^{5a-12}=\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${8}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{({8}^{2})}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{8}^{2(a+4)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & ={8}^{-2(a+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(8)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-12 & =-2(a+4) \\\\ 5a-12 & =-2a-8 \\\\ 7a & =4 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100253d3-1c8e-57f5-be50-9c4c3ffc3295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244460} {:answer/id #uuid "29317010-3e68-558c-b204-1ce0cfcaf5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244675} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb8bdf6-3a9e-5b2d-9194-f2292be44e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245569} {:answer/id #uuid "90ef1862-26cc-5a7d-a4ac-7e180c9b1c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245579} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a3faf2-39fa-57b9-9c7f-56f054e3f237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "100253d3-1c8e-57f5-be50-9c4c3ffc3295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244460} :answer-field/id #uuid "248fe733-e8f7-5a88-838b-554e51bb6fff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f10f6ac8-1846-5184-add3-415104706f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-110537" :question/problem "If ${4}^{k-1}={64}^{2k}$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${4}^{3},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{k-1} & ={64}^{2k} \\\\ {4}^{k-1} & ={({4}^{3})}^{2k} \\\\ {4}^{k-1} & ={4}^{6k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-1 & =6k \\\\ -1 & =5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2557e134-8fec-5ab3-960c-a57b00468ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295468} {:answer/id #uuid "7121766c-6e6c-50fb-b405-39872e4a3675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295889} {:answer/id #uuid "743f0b86-9493-57b7-8cd7-106216e5985e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295903} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae040a7-2233-56cf-a154-f9c66ec85341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295938} {:answer/id #uuid "b203df57-3c29-5af0-a0c9-dd565b91f9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7121766c-6e6c-50fb-b405-39872e4a3675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295889} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9dcf296-3fab-54e0-a5ef-5bc12cd62f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297052}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ea2dd05-d2d3-5da1-bad5-e07ed434ba91" :question/math-academy-id "q-3103" :question/problem "If ${5}^{2a-5}=\\frac{1}{{25}^{a+1}}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $25$ as ${5}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{25}^{a+1}} \\\\ {5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{({5}^{2})}^{a+1}} \\\\ {5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{2(a+1)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a-5} & ={5}^{-2(a+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5 & =-2(a+1) \\\\ 2a-5 & =-2a-2 \\\\ 4a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223c177a-d9d7-56cd-ad4a-3477d50d4c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333296} {:answer/id #uuid "323a913a-973d-59e1-bf67-21fa4537869b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333454} {:answer/id #uuid "6503bcd7-e33d-594d-9276-3205ef82e188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333907} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f071d-aa78-50ae-b1ac-3a53253f9868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334620} {:answer/id #uuid "d5be51db-4b95-5f18-bdba-64d3e9539848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f071d-aa78-50ae-b1ac-3a53253f9868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334620} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f34fa5-49b8-5d1e-8a10-c2a061a8bc48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c8c6efa-1e45-5ac2-8d8a-2e0aeaebd494" :question/math-academy-id "q-66863" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where ${4}^{x+4}={16}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $16$ as ${4}^{2},$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+4} & ={16}^{x} \\\\ {4}^{x+4} & ={({4}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {4}^{x+4} & ={4}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =2x \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4655e0e1-97d5-5387-82f5-2a0060583577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333640} {:answer/id #uuid "57bb8d9b-280e-513c-acdb-1014d45f33b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333791} {:answer/id #uuid "8840f7da-ffe1-5988-a8d4-352d29fbe330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334194} {:answer/id #uuid "b242132e-1e99-5659-9c4b-f06d8ee0a1f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334553} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ce3b19-3ccd-51e4-9d8f-e8979ffcdfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7ce3b19-3ccd-51e4-9d8f-e8979ffcdfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335160} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdd81fd-15d7-5d1a-9dc4-6f1226cd81c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335327}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e3e80cb-8e1b-5722-8654-94f1e02c6077" :question/math-academy-id "q-66872" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where ${5}^{m-10}={125}^{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $125$ as ${5}^{3},$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{m-10} & ={125}^{m} \\\\ {5}^{m-10} & ={({5}^{3})}^{m} \\\\ {5}^{m-10} & ={5}^{3m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-10 & =3m \\\\ -2m & =10 \\\\ m & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115048 :question/id #uuid "e73675af-973f-583e-9909-0eb9630fdcc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4161" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={\\color{red}3}^2$ and $27 = {\\color{red}3}^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(3x)}={3}^{3(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3x) & =3(x+2) \\\\ 6x & =3x+6 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cbf6a27-5abf-56f0-96e5-2773ce7b62d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114596} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c8f896-da87-5acf-9e69-d5217e45288c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114597} {:answer/id #uuid "d540f591-4bda-5ebc-ab94-b52d44948574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114598} {:answer/id #uuid "454abe14-6e0c-5bf5-bef1-9a0c39ff0237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114599} {:answer/id #uuid "7e914151-9093-5276-be19-33078b973cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114600}] :answer-field/id #uuid "33d72840-8e8c-5485-86f8-0a2546a312ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114595}] :question/id #uuid "9114b5ca-997d-5e0e-9ea5-1895871cf130" :question/math-academy-id "q-66788" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $4^{x + 1} = 8^{2x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23547e2f-726d-5dfc-b054-2ce0415151fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114608} {:answer/id #uuid "3498e3e5-6e90-5be9-bb54-42fcd765fdfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114609} {:answer/id #uuid "455fc955-6206-569b-97bb-4995d8be74ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114610} {:answer/id #uuid "59e3a8bb-f7e0-5a24-a1b4-7b6a7b3efeee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114611} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3a0735-31d0-5b62-ac91-4e7b4e6178fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114612}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3418477b-2989-515c-b2f5-0b9a0f59ed2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114607}] :question/id #uuid "ef58fde3-29e4-575b-952a-f0b7b949c63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66893" :question/problem "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation $16^{d - 2} = 32^{d + 1}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233030 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62c0b89d-1d8a-5723-8092-97486825267c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100459" :question/problem "If ${216}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{36})}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}})}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233866 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c366f3c-26d4-5a78-b6c8-77d9d1b20627" :question/math-academy-id "q-3362" :question/problem "If ${27}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{81})}^{-2n+8},$ then $n=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $27={3}^{3}$ and $81={3}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{3})}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}})}^{-2n+8}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the power of quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4(-2n+8)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}={3}^{-4(-2n+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2n+2) & =-4(-2n+8) \\\\ 6n+6 & =8n-32 \\\\ 2n & =38 \\\\ n & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e6c4527-389f-53df-a817-0ed1f3abbe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236210} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bbfad5-b2b8-56ce-9add-a4ac75631348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237000} {:answer/id #uuid "cd32b9be-49e7-5180-9502-60b22eb68cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237118} {:answer/id #uuid "e390ede5-d86c-5365-9c30-858c8