0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=2.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3df6cd2-68c7-5027-b94c-cebc3edbefd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398984} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7403d1-9523-59ef-892c-8762070fc770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398985} {:answer/id #uuid "43b3ca27-4022-5e94-91a9-05d77b4b0c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398986} {:answer/id #uuid "321a43c8-209e-53e7-bddf-716f4dd735c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398987} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f1972b-cdc9-5bbd-bf68-8c034ebe8712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "321a43c8-209e-53e7-bddf-716f4dd735c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398987} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce520123-e722-5fc8-b4b5-0ce963a1fe4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398983}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b1df7fe-03a7-50e4-93e7-ab9ba910308e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74650" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+6y=4 \\\\  & x+(k+1)y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+6y=4 \\\\  & (k-2)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $2x+6y=4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0250b550-0b7c-5c05-b8f5-33da7345f93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417069} {:answer/id #uuid "0e30254f-fef5-5030-ad74-62323657f390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417070} {:answer/id #uuid "32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417071} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0d5bd9-5c97-5445-afdc-58640864c07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417074} {:answer/id #uuid "e23cbcca-7092-5855-aeeb-dd2de6061a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417071} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b28742-5ee2-597a-8428-7062724625e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417079}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6deb5ca1-ae5b-5721-895d-6efa8ac5fdfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131730" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+2y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (2-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{2}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17602323-8777-51fe-9a70-fac173438e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425938} {:answer/id #uuid "3745dd5b-bc8f-5c5f-bfbb-0a70d0ee8eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425940} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1862b9-030a-5755-b668-e59ca4c9e456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425944} {:answer/id #uuid "a1294db0-0da9-5a02-b9e5-791ef34dcf11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425946} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6dc1f0-4ead-53bf-8e9f-9b57805aa51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6dc1f0-4ead-53bf-8e9f-9b57805aa51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425947} :answer-field/id #uuid "80130b9b-78a0-54cc-bf84-e036ce6c2ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425948}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db005b4e-86cd-5194-b2fd-10ca4ee59c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-42021" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=3 \\\\ -3x-(k+1)y=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}]}
